      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m worried the `vma_slice_off(vma)` check turns mmaps that work today into errors. Since aosp/4368055, `remap_pfn_range()` maps a 4KB VMA with `vm_slice_off !\u003d 0` from that slice, and several callers bound such mappings for it: `system_heap_mmap()` remaps `vma_kernel_page_bytes()` chunks (aosp/4367597), the DMA mmaps check `vma_kernel_pages()` (aosp/4368058) and `vma_exceeds_object()` (aosp/4368011), and `p3s_vm_iomap_memory()` checks `vma_file_offset()` (aosp/4368009).\n\nWith this check, a 4KB process that mmaps a system heap dma-buf or a DMA buffer at an offset that isn\u0027t 16KB-aligned gets `-EINVAL`, while `vm_map_pages()` (e.g. `iommu_dma_mmap()` of a non-contiguous buffer) still maps it.\n\nThe tail trim looks right to me, and as far as I can see it doesn\u0027t need this condition, since `vma_slice_offset()` already counts from `vm_slice_off`. Would it be OK to drop it?\n\nIf it\u0027s meant to protect drivers that ignore `vm_slice_off`, such as `uio_mmap_physical()`, could the commit message say so, and could the refusal go through `p3s_4k_unsupported()` (aosp/4366134) so that it shows up in the log? The slice handling in aosp/4367597, aosp/4368009 and aosp/4368011 would then no longer be reachable through `remap_pfn_range()`.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "ac03d343a247a96cb219f705f66184ab63272f40",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
